# Claude Code

- **Never publish through the Artifact tool** (claude.ai artifacts). Multiple
  Claude accounts are in play and the web login often doesn't match the CLI
  account, so artifact URLs are dead ends. HTML/visual output is still wanted —
  write it to a local file and hand over the path (or `open` it, where the
  content rules allow auto-opening).
- The push approval rule is enforced by `~/.claude/git-push-guard.sh`.
- Never push from a Claude worktree under `.claude/worktrees/`; land its work
  through the parent checkout.

## Worktrees

- Claude Code worktree isolation (`EnterWorktree`, `--worktree`, and Agent
  `isolation: "worktree"`) must branch from the parent session's `HEAD`.
  `worktree.baseRef: "head"` is configured globally, but verify the worktree
  `HEAD` matches the parent before making changes. If it does not, fetch the
  parent checkout's `HEAD` and rebase onto `FETCH_HEAD`; stop on conflicts.
- In an isolated worktree, resolve its top level first and use absolute paths
  under that worktree for every Read/Edit/Write operation. Claude Code can
  otherwise reroute relative paths to the parent checkout through the shared git
  directory. Stop if isolation silently produced the parent path.
- Before finishing isolated work, prove the parent checkout has no leaked edits
  or commits and that the work is present on the worktree branch. The parent
  session must verify this again before landing the branch.
- Prefer sequential edits in the main tree for small or tightly coupled work.
  Split parallel edit agents by disjoint file ownership.

## OpenSpec

- The main workspace is for chatting, speccing, brainstorming, and non-spec
  implementation. A sizeable OpenSpec change is implemented by a fresh Claude
  session native to its dedicated herdr worktree.
- For a sizeable natural-language request to implement or apply an OpenSpec
  change, use `rjm:opsx-worktree` by default. A literal `/opsx:apply`, an
  explicit in-place request, unavailable herdr, or a non-git directory uses the
  in-place flow instead.
- The worktree implementer stops with a clean, green, fast-forward-ready branch.
  It never pushes or moves local `main`; the operator lands it from the parent
  checkout.

## Model selection

Three tiers, chosen by what the work is (operator, 2026-09-03; replaces the
earlier "Fable for synthesis, switch to Opus deferred" note). The rule applies
wherever a model is picked: `cl --fable | --opus | --sonnet` when launching a
session, `model:` on the Agent tool, and per-phase `opts.model` in a Workflow.

- **Fable** — orchestrating, planning and speccing (proposals, designs,
  rulings, handoff seeds that plan); very complex implementation (cross-cutting,
  a new crate or protocol change, several subsystems at once); the single
  research-synthesis bottleneck; and the workflows that need it. Never a
  fan-out default.
- **Opus** — implementation, and ordinary research synthesis.
- **Sonnet** — cheaper research, and the high-volume fan-out phases of a
  workflow: scoping, retrieval, adversarial verification.
- **A fix is picked by complexity, not by default** (operator, 2026-09-07,
  mid QA walk). **Sonnet** for a one-file or one-line change whose brief names
  the files and quotes the rule; **Opus** for an ordinary fix — a spec sentence,
  a gate, a handful of files; **Fable** only when the fix is cross-cutting and
  its shape is unruled, which is usually a proposal wearing a fix's name. The
  dispatcher says which in the brief's first line.
- **The tier is not the effort.** `effortLevel: xhigh` in `settings.json` is
  inherited by every subagent, so an unset Sonnet one-liner reasons as hard as
  Fable speccing. The `fix` agent (`~/.claude/agents/fix.md`, dispatched as
  `subagent_type: fix`, `model:` overriding its Sonnet default) sets
  `effort: high`; the orchestrator keeps `xhigh`. Measured 2026-09-07
  (`unspoke/docs/where-the-minutes-go.md`): effort is ~20% of a turn's cost and
  the turn count is the rest — a fix is one loop, one verification line, never
  `pnpm check`.
- A Workflow chooses per phase, never one model for the whole script. The
  bundled `deep-research` workflow is read-only; reproduce this tiering in a
  custom script when needed. Fable is selectable as an agent/subagent model
  (`model: fable`; confirmed 2026-07-21).
- When a skill launches or dispatches, it names which tier it chose and why in
  one line; a launcher that cannot tell (a change whose Impact is unread) reads
  first rather than defaulting.
